// Every LITERAL FORM that can stand as a `println` argument, in one carrier.
//
// Why this fixture exists (wave M3a, 2026-09-09): lowering `println` asks each
// argument for its type, and the channel records NONE for a literal -- nobody
// ever asked, because the only consumer was a printer that knew the form in
// advance. The first edition of the lowering answered `str` for every literal
// and judged the FIRST leaf; the corpus never caught it, because every println
// in the corpus spells a string label and passes the rest as expressions. So
// the bare literal forms had no carrier at all -- this is it.
//
// The negated case is the one a hand-written list would miss: a negative
// literal arrives as `[Minus, lit]`, so the token that decides the type is the
// LAST leaf, not the first.
//
// A CHAR was absent on purpose until registry #1060: `println('z')` passed
// novac's checker and then ICEd in emission -- `printer_of` (emit_c/emit_expr.nv)
// had no printer for the char family, though the runtime's `nova_print_char`
// already existed. The oracle prints `z` (measured 2026-09-09 by building and
// running it), so the program was legal Nova and novac's ICE was a DEFECT, not
// a subset boundary. Fixed by adding the missing match arm; this is now its
// carrier, as the earlier comment promised it would be.
module println_literals.pos_1

fn main() {
    println("a string")
    println(7)
    println(-7)
    println(2.5)
    println(-2.5)
    println(true)
    println('z')
    println("label", 7, -2.5, true)
}
